{{!-- GM MOVES — the playbook's first spread: the basic list, then Exploration, then Homefront.
      Reference only. Nothing here rolls and nothing here is stored; the lists come from
      module/gm-toolkit/gm-moves.js.

      `class="tab moves"` is load-bearing, not a copy-paste leftover, though NOT for the
      reason the same class carries elsewhere. What it earns is the tab's padding, the 14px gap
      between move groups, and the fold caret's box — all three written against `.tab.moves`
      plus `.stonetop-move-group`. Nothing unwanted rides along: the learned-move filter is
      `.tab.moves.hide-unselected`, and only the character sheet ever adds that class.

      The ENTRIES are deliberately not the bordered move card, and they say so by wearing their
      own classes rather than the shared card's. They used to carry `stonetop-item` and its
      three children and the stylesheet took the card back off again declaration by
      declaration; a reference entry shares nothing with a card but its font, so it is cheaper
      to describe than to undo.

      `data-tab="moves"` is also what earns the rail's move glyph — the icon table in
      stonetop.css is one flat list keyed by data-tab, with no per-sheet scope.

      Each section is BOXED in its own `.stonetop-move-group`. The fold walk claims a heading's
      following siblings until it meets the next heading (utils/section-editing.js), so three
      headings in one flat run would let the Basic caret swallow Exploration and Homefront. --}}
<div class="tab moves" data-group="primary" data-tab="moves">
	<section class="sheet-tab stonetop-gm-toolkit-moves">

		{{#each stonetop.moveSections}}
		<div class="stonetop-move-group">
			{{!-- The tab's own name lives in a tooltip now that the rail is icon-only, so these
			      headings are the only place the page says what it is.

			      `randomize` is the die beside the note: it draws one move out of THIS section
			      and whispers it to the GM (module/gm-toolkit/random-gm-move.js). Keyed by the
			      section's own key, so the three buttons need no wiring of their own. --}}
			{{> "stonetop.section-heading" title=title note=note collapse=collapseId
			                              randomize=key randomizeTitle=randomizeTitle}}
			<ol class="items-list">
				{{#each moves}}
				{{!-- `data-move` is how the randomizer finds the row it just drew, to light it up
				      for five seconds (gm-move-drawer.js `GmMoveDrawer#spinTo`). The NAME rather
				      than the index, because the index would be the position in a list this
				      template is free to reorder while the handler kept matching by number.

				      ONE BLURB, not a summary and then a paragraph. The line under the name is the
				      book's own first sentence (`blurb.lead`), and opening the entry reveals the
				      rest of that same sentence's paragraph INLINE, right where the reader's eye
				      already is. Nothing on screen moves; the text simply carries on. That is why
				      the lead is the book's words and not our `gloss`: our line would have to be
				      re-read and then replaced, and the join would show.

				      The blurb IS the disclosure button, so the caret sits at the end of whatever
				      is currently shown: immediately after the label while shut, and after the
				      last word of the paragraph once open. A caret pinned after the lead alone
				      would end up mid-sentence the moment the rest arrived.

				      Space and Enter work without a keydown handler of ours because it is a real
				      <button>, and `aria-expanded` says which way it is. `aria-controls` is
				      deliberately absent: it would need an id per entry, unique across three
				      sections and thirty entries, and the panel is the button's own next sibling.
				      The tooltip comes from the SECTION (`../`), where the strings are localized.

				      The panel below holds the rest of what Book I prints: any further paragraphs,
				      the soft/hard line where the book gives one, the examples of play VERBATIM,
				      and the page. It is `hidden` at render, always. The tab is a scannable
				      reference list first, and thirty open entries is a chapter. --}}
				<li class="stonetop-gm-move" data-move="{{name}}">
					<strong class="stonetop-gm-move-name">{{name}}</strong>
					<button type="button" class="stonetop-gm-move-toggle" aria-expanded="false"
					        data-tooltip="{{../expandTitle}}"><span class="stonetop-gm-move-lead">{{blurb.lead}}</span>{{#if blurb.rest}}<span class="stonetop-gm-move-rest" hidden> {{blurb.rest}}</span>{{/if}}</button>
					<div class="stonetop-gm-move-book" hidden>
						{{#each blurb.paragraphs}}
						<p class="stonetop-gm-move-detail">{{this}}</p>
						{{/each}}
						{{#if blurb.hardness}}
						<p class="stonetop-gm-move-hardness">{{blurb.hardness}}</p>
						{{/if}}
						{{!-- Every example is a quotation from the book, and is marked up as one
						      rather than styled to look like one: it is somebody else's text, in
						      somebody else's voice, naming the book's own sample cast. --}}
						{{#each examples}}
						<blockquote class="stonetop-gm-move-example">{{this}}</blockquote>
						{{/each}}
						{{#if pageCites}}
						<p class="stonetop-gm-move-page">{{> "stonetop.book-page-cite" cites=pageCites}}</p>
						{{/if}}
					</div>
				</li>
				{{/each}}
			</ol>
		</div>
		{{/each}}

	</section>
</div>
